module.exports = /*#__PURE__*/JSON.parse('{"id":"avl-tree-deletion","title":"Step-by-Step AVL Deletion","subjectId":"intermediate-dsa-1","nextLessonId":"avl-tree-implementation","prevLessonId":"avl-tree-insertion","content":[{"type":"paragraph","text":"Hello! Welcome back. In our previous lesson, we learned how to insert nodes into an AVL tree and balance them instantly. Today, we will study the opposite operation: **deleting a node** from an AVL tree while keeping it perfectly balanced! You will see how removing a node can cause a chain reaction of height changes, and how we fix it step-by-step."},{"type":"heading","id":"connect-to-previous","level":2,"text":"1. Connecting to What You Already Know"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Do you remember how we deleted a node in a standard Binary Search Tree (BST)? We had three simple cases depending on the node we wanted to remove: if it was a leaf node (no children), if it had only one child, or if it had two children. We also know how to perform rotations to fix imbalances (+2 or -2) in AVL trees. Today, we are simply going to combine these two concepts!"},{"type":"heading","id":"three-deletion-cases","level":2,"text":"2. The Three Deletion Cases"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Before looking at a complex tree, let\'s trace the three cases of node deletion based on child counts:"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"· **Case 1: Node with No Children (Leaf Node)**: This is the easiest case. We simply disconnect the node from its parent. For example, if we delete Node 8 (which has no children), Node 7\'s right pointer becomes null."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"· **Case 2: Node with Single Child**: If the node has one child, we bypass it. We point its parent directly to its child. For example, if we delete Node 7 (which has only Node 4 as its child), Node 11\'s left pointer points directly to Node 4."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"· **Case 3: Node with Left & Right Subtrees (Two Children)**: We cannot simply delete the node because it would break the connections of both subtrees. Instead, we find the node\'s **inorder successor** (the smallest node in its right subtree). We copy the successor\'s value into the target node, then recursively delete the successor node\'s original position."},{"type":"heading","id":"deletion-algorithm-steps","level":2,"text":"3. Step-by-Step Deletion Algorithm"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Every time you delete a value from an AVL tree, follow this exact sequence of steps:"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"1. **Standard BST Deletion**: Traverse recursively to find the node to delete. Once found, apply the correct child case:\\n· **No children (Leaf)**: Delete the node and return `nullptr` to the parent.\\n· **One child**: Store the child in a temporary pointer, delete the node, and return the child pointer to the parent.\\n· **Two children**: Locate the inorder successor (smallest node in the right subtree). Copy the successor value to the current node, then recursively delete that successor node from the right subtree."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"2. **Update Height (Backtracking)**: As the recursion unwinds back up the tree, update the height of the current ancestor node: `height = 1 + std::max(getHeightOfNode(left), getHeightOfNode(right))`."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"3. **Calculate Balance Factor (BF)**: For the current ancestor node, compute: `balance = getBalanceOfNode(node)`."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"4. **Check for Imbalance**: If `balance` is within `[-1, 0, 1]`, the subtree is balanced. If `balance > 1` or `balance < -1`, the node is unbalanced and requires rebalancing."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"5. **Perform the Correct Rotation** (Unlike insertion, we inspect the child\'s balance factor directly to choose the rotation):"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"· **Left-Left (LL) Case**: If `balance > 1` and `getBalanceOfNode(left_child) >= 0`, perform a single **Right Rotation** (`LLRoation`).\\n· **Left-Right (LR) Case**: If `balance > 1` and `getBalanceOfNode(left_child) < 0`, perform an LR Double Rotation (`LRrotation`).\\n· **Right-Right (RR) Case**: If `balance < -1` and `getBalanceOfNode(right_child) <= 0`, perform a single **Left Rotation** (`RRrotation`).\\n· **Right-Left (RL) Case**: If `balance < -1` and `getBalanceOfNode(right_child) > 0`, perform an RL Double Rotation (`RLrotation`)."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Here is the complete C++ AVL deletion template containing the helper functions, four rotations, inorder successor search, and recursive deletion backtracking logic:"},{"type":"code","language":"cpp","code":"class Node {\\npublic:\\n    int data;\\n    Node* left;\\n    Node* right;\\n    int height;\\n    \\n    Node(int value) {\\n        data = value;\\n        left = nullptr;\\n        right = nullptr;\\n        height = 1;\\n    }\\n};\\n\\n// Safe helper to get a node\'s height (nullptr-safe)\\nint getHeightOfNode(Node* node) {\\n    if (node == nullptr) return 0;\\n    return node->height;\\n}\\n\\n// Safe helper to calculate a node\'s balance factor\\nint getBalanceOfNode(Node* node) {\\n    if (node == nullptr) return 0;\\n    return getHeightOfNode(node->left) - getHeightOfNode(node->right);\\n}\\n\\n// 1. LL Case: Right Rotation around y\\nNode* LLRoation(Node* y) {\\n    Node* x = y->left;\\n    Node* T2 = x->right;\\n    \\n    x->right = y;\\n    y->left = T2;\\n    \\n    y->height = 1 + max(getHeightOfNode(y->left), getHeightOfNode(y->right));\\n    x->height = 1 + max(getHeightOfNode(x->left), getHeightOfNode(x->right));\\n    \\n    return x;\\n}\\n\\n// 2. RR Case: Left Rotation around y\\nNode* RRrotation(Node* y) {\\n    Node* x = y->right;\\n    Node* T2 = x->left;\\n    \\n    x->left = y;\\n    y->right = T2;\\n    \\n    y->height = 1 + max(getHeightOfNode(y->left), getHeightOfNode(y->right));\\n    x->height = 1 + max(getHeightOfNode(x->left), getHeightOfNode(x->right));\\n    \\n    return x;\\n}\\n\\n// 3. LR Case: Double Rotation\\nNode* LRrotation(Node* root) {\\n    root->left = RRrotation(root->left);\\n    root = LLRoation(root);\\n    return root;\\n}\\n\\n// 4. RL Case: Double Rotation\\nNode* RLrotation(Node* root) {\\n    root->right = LLRoation(root->right);\\n    root = RRrotation(root);\\n    return root;\\n}\\n\\n// Helper to find the inorder successor (node with minimum value in right subtree)\\nNode* minValueNode(Node* node) {\\n    Node* current = node;\\n    while (current->left != nullptr)\\n        current = current->left;\\n    return current;\\n}\\n\\n// Main AVL Deletion Function\\nNode* deleteNode(Node* root, int key) {\\n    if (root == nullptr) return root;\\n    \\n    if (key < root->data) {\\n        root->left = deleteNode(root->left, key);\\n    } else if (key > root->data) {\\n        root->right = deleteNode(root->right, key);\\n    } else {\\n        if ((root->left == nullptr) || (root->right == nullptr)) {\\n            Node* temp = root->left ? root->left : root->right;\\n            if (temp == nullptr) {\\n                temp = root;\\n                root = nullptr;\\n            } else {\\n                *root = *temp;\\n            }\\n            delete temp;\\n        } else {\\n            Node* temp = minValueNode(root->right);\\n            root->data = temp->data;\\n            root->right = deleteNode(root->right, temp->data);\\n        }\\n    }\\n    \\n    if (root == nullptr) return root;\\n    \\n    root->height = 1 + max(getHeightOfNode(root->left), getHeightOfNode(root->right));\\n    int balance = getBalanceOfNode(root);\\n    \\n    if (balance > 1 && getBalanceOfNode(root->left) >= 0)\\n        return LLRoation(root);\\n    if (balance > 1 && getBalanceOfNode(root->left) < 0)\\n        return LRrotation(root);\\n    if (balance < -1 && getBalanceOfNode(root->right) <= 0)\\n        return RRrotation(root);\\n    if (balance < -1 && getBalanceOfNode(root->right) > 0)\\n        return RLrotation(root);\\n        \\n    return root;\\n}"},{"type":"heading","id":"interactive-deletion-simulator","level":2,"text":"4. Interactive Deletion Simulator"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Let\'s construct our AVL tree and delete these nodes one by one: **8, 7, 11, 14, 17**. Use the interactive whiteboard simulator below to follow along with each deletion and see how the heights, balance factors, and rebalancing rotations are calculated!"},{"type":"drawing","id":"avl-deletion-drawing"},{"type":"heading","id":"deletion-complexity","level":2,"text":"5. Complexity Analysis"},{"type":"complexity_card","timeComplexity":"O(log N)","timeExplanation":"Searching for the node to delete takes O(log N) steps. Walking back up to the root to update heights and perform rotations also takes O(log N) steps, since the height is at most log N.","spaceComplexity":"O(log N)","spaceExplanation":"The recursion stack uses memory proportional to the height of the tree, which is O(log N)."},{"type":"heading","id":"practice-questions","level":2,"text":"6. Practice Questions"},{"type":"mcq","question":"What is the inorder successor of a node in a BST/AVL tree?","options":["The largest node in its left subtree.","The smallest node in its right subtree.","The parent node.","The leftmost leaf node in the entire tree."],"correctAnswerIndex":1},{"type":"mcq","question":"Which node became unbalanced after deleting Node 17 in our sequence?","options":["Node 16 (Root)","Node 19","Node 53","Node 12"],"correctAnswerIndex":1},{"type":"mcq","question":"What rotation is triggered to balance the tree after deleting Node 17?","options":["Single Right Rotation (LL Case)","Single Left Rotation (RR Case)","Double Left-Right Rotation (LR Case)","Double Right-Left Rotation (RL Case)"],"correctAnswerIndex":1},{"type":"heading","id":"summary-section","level":2,"text":"7. Summary"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"To wrap up: AVL tree deletion works by performing a standard BST deletion and then backtracking up to the root to fix heights and execute rotations if any node\'s Balance Factor becomes +2 or -2. By tracing the sequential deletion of 8, 7, 11, 14, and 17 on a tree containing 20 and 60, we saw how an RR Left Rotation is triggered in practice. Next, we will translate this logic into complete C++ code from scratch!"}]}');

